{
  "concept": "A boat's distance from shore can be recovered from angle measurements only if a known baseline exists — either the observer's height above the water (single observer) or the separation between two observers (triangulation) — which is the geometric seed of stereo depth estimation.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "White sky band at top, cyan sea filling the lower-right region, ochre/brown land mass rising from the left",
    "Horizontal horizon line separating sky from sea, extending across the full panel",
    "Observer 1: open white circle with black outline (the eye/camera), placed on the cliff top in single-observer mode and on the shore in two-observer mode",
    "Observer 2: second open white circle lower on the shore, present only in the two-observer mode",
    "Sailboat icon (hull outline plus two triangular sails and mast) floating on the sea at the right",
    "Vertical baseline segment from observer down to the water line, labelled 'h' (single-observer mode)",
    "Slanted baseline segment between the two observers, labelled 't' (two-observer mode)",
    "Horizon reference ray drawn horizontally from the observer's eye toward the right",
    "Line of sight from observer 1 to the boat",
    "Line of sight from observer 2 to the boat (two-observer mode)",
    "Depression angle arc labelled 'α' between the horizon ray and the line of sight at observer 1",
    "Second angle arc labelled 'α' at the boat, marking the alternate/equal angle to the base line (single-observer mode)",
    "Angle arc labelled 'β' at observer 2 between the baseline and its line of sight (two-observer mode)",
    "Grey square right-angle marker at the foot of the height segment (single-observer mode) and at the perpendicular foot on the baseline (two-observer mode)",
    "Base line running from the foot of the height to the boat, labelled 'd?' — the unknown distance",
    "Numeric readout panel showing h or t, the measured angles, and the computed distance d",
    "Faint uncertainty wedge (shaded cone) around the estimated boat position showing sensitivity of d to a small angular error",
    "Legend / mode caption naming the active method"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "methodToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Both distance estimates are the same triangle argument; only the choice of known baseline changes — vertical height versus horizontal separation between two eyes.",
      "label": "Measurement method",
      "options": ["Single observer (height baseline)", "Two observers (separation baseline)"],
      "default": "Single observer (height baseline)"
    },
    {
      "id": "observerHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "For a fixed boat, raising the observer increases the depression angle α; since d = h / tan(α), a taller vantage point makes the angle larger and the distance estimate far more stable.",
      "label": "Observer height h (m)",
      "range": [2, 120, 1],
      "default": 40
    },
    {
      "id": "baselineSeparation",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Widening the gap between the two observers opens the triangle at the boat, making the intersection of the two sight lines sharply defined instead of nearly parallel.",
      "label": "Observer separation t (m)",
      "range": [5, 200, 5],
      "default": 60
    },
    {
      "id": "boatDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As the boat moves away, both α and the angle between the two sight lines shrink toward zero — depth information degrades quadratically with distance for any fixed baseline.",
      "label": "True boat distance d (m)",
      "range": [50, 1500, 10],
      "default": 400
    },
    {
      "id": "angleNoise",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A small error in the measured angle translates into a large error in distance when the baseline is short or the boat is far — this is the precision limit of all triangulation, including stereo.",
      "label": "Angle measurement error (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 2, 0.1],
      "default": 0.3
    },
    {
      "id": "boatDrag",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "Moving the boat by hand shows the rays, the angles and the computed distance updating together as one rigid geometric construction."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One eye, one ray — no depth",
      "narration": "A single sight line from the observer to the boat is consistent with a boat at any distance along that ray. Nothing about the ray alone fixes d.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height baseline)", "observerHeight": 40, "boatDistance": 400, "angleNoise": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Height as the hidden baseline",
      "narration": "Adding the known height h and the horizon as a horizontal reference closes the triangle: the depression angle α reappears at the boat, and d = h / tan α.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height baseline)", "observerHeight": 40, "boatDistance": 400, "angleNoise": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Climb higher, see farther, measure better",
      "narration": "Raising the observer from a low shore to a cliff top makes α much larger for the same boat, so the same angular error now costs far less distance error.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height baseline)", "observerHeight": 110, "boatDistance": 400, "angleNoise": 0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Swap the baseline: two observers",
      "narration": "Switch methods. Now the known quantity is the separation t between two vantage points, and the boat sits at the intersection of two sight lines — classic triangulation.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (separation baseline)", "baselineSeparation": 60, "boatDistance": 400, "angleNoise": 0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Baseline length sets precision",
      "narration": "Shrink t and the two rays become nearly parallel — the uncertainty wedge stretches out along the line of sight. Widen t and the intersection snaps tight. This is exactly why stereo cameras want a wide baseline.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (separation baseline)", "baselineSeparation": 15, "boatDistance": 900, "angleNoise": 0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Far objects defeat both methods",
      "narration": "Push the boat to the horizon with any baseline: α and the vergence angle both collapse, and the distance estimate becomes wildly uncertain — depth from angles always fails at long range.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (separation baseline)", "baselineSeparation": 200, "boatDistance": 1500, "angleNoise": 0.5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Angles alone never give distance — depth comes from combining an angle with a known baseline, and whether that baseline is your height above the water or the gap between two eyes, longer baseline and larger angle mean a more certain distance.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single wide panel that swaps content by mode, replacing the original two panels. Background: white sky on top, a straight black horizon line, cyan (turquoise) sea filling the lower right, and an ochre/brown irregular landmass entering from the left with a hand-drawn wavy silhouette; in single-observer mode the land rises into a tall dark-brown peak, in two-observer mode it is a lower sloping shore. All geometry is thin black lines on top. Observers are small white circles with heavy black outlines. The sailboat is a simple black-outlined white icon (hull plus two sails) sitting on the water at the right, its position driven by the distance slider. Single-observer mode: horizontal horizon ray from the eye to the right, a sight line down to the boat with arc labelled α near the eye, a vertical segment labelled 'h' from eye to the water level with a grey right-angle square at its foot, the base line from that foot to the boat labelled 'd?', and a second α arc at the boat. Two-observer mode: two circles on the shore joined by a slanted segment labelled 't' with a grey right-angle marker on it, sight lines from each observer to the boat forming a thin triangle, α arc at the upper observer, β arc at the lower observer, and the upper sight line labelled 'd?'. Add a small semi-transparent red/orange uncertainty wedge around the boat whose length grows with angle error and distance and shrinks with baseline, plus a compact readout box (monospace) listing h or t, α, β, and the computed d. Labels use serif italic-style math glyphs matching the original: h, t, d?, α, β."
}

